{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795297275,"nonce":"i39pp0hJtGreZ4WWqCaw/9qTXOc=","message":"Fix committed locally as a777c072c79512144dd631be57caf4aa7fde8687 (not pushed). Left open until it lands; follow-ups below.\n\nWhat it does: the LLVM emitter turns an innermost, call-free loop that counts up against an invariant bound into two loops. The outer loop polls once per strip of 256 and sets `end = min(bound, i + 256)`; the inner loop tests `i \u003c end` and holds only the body. The AIR, the interpreter, OSR and Cranelift are untouched. A loop that carries a floating-point value keeps the single polled loop. ASH_NESTED_STRIP=0 turns the split off. Code: crates/ash/src/llvm/function/nested_strip.rs.\n\nhxn's SoA kernel, ns per element, default hybrid:\n\n    Mac (arm64)   NativeArray 0.589 -\u003e 0.221   hl.Bytes 0.556 -\u003e 0.101   Float32Buffer 0.58 -\u003e 0.103\n    NUC (x86)     NativeArray 0.56  -\u003e 0.112   hl.Bytes 0.57  -\u003e 0.058   Float32Buffer 0.58 -\u003e 0.109\n    hxn's hxcpp number: 0.22 (Mac)\n\nThat equals the ceiling measured with ASH_FIBER_POLLS=0. The poll is still emitted and still fires once per strip. Results are numerically identical.\n\nChecks:\n- Conformance, 1069/1069 cases: hybrid with every function through LLVM at its first call, and AOT.\n- TestNestedStrip: 13 trip counts including 0, 1, 255, 256, 257, step 2, negative start, bounds at the ends of Int, break and continue, a call in the body, count-down, 2D, a bound the loop changes. interp, hybrid, jit cranelift, jit llvm, hybrid llvm threshold 1, and ASH_NESTED_STRIP=0 all print the same text. The integration test also asserts that the vectorized loop count rises with nesting and the poll count does not fall.\n- clippy -D warnings clean.\n\nNUC A/B against the same tree without the nested strip, interleaved, 29 benchmarks in hybrid-auto and full-jit: nothing moved beyond noise. The call benchmarks that sank the AIR-level split in 9d82d77 did not regress: closure_call -4.4%, deltablue -5.5%, method_call -2.6%, binary_trees -0.7%. Physics step: 20 chains -3.6%, 80 chains +0.2%.\n\nTwo things found on the way:\n- spectralnorm cost about +3% in hybrid at first. Its hot loops carry floating-point sums, which LLVM cannot vectorize, and the split added about 3 ms of LLVM compile time per function. Those loops now keep the single polled loop, and it is back to parity.\n- fannkuchredux reads +2.8% median in hybrid in several runs, with the sign flipping between runs and no cost with LLVM pinned. Not explained; I could not tie it to compile time. Treat as noise until a quieter machine says otherwise.\n\nStill open:\n- A cheaper check for whether LLVM will vectorize a loop (indirect indexes, loop-carried memory dependences), so nesting does not cost compile time where it cannot pay.\n- AOT performance was not measured, only AOT conformance.\n- hl.NativeArray data starts 24 bytes into the object, so 16-byte vector accesses are misaligned; NativeArray stays about 2x slower than an aligned hl.Bytes buffer.\n- Only counted loops that count up. Count-down loops, loops with a call, and loops whose bound changes keep the single polled form.","files":null}]}